    "body": "### Problem\n\nRunning :read {file} emits a Progress event with:\n\n{\n  data = vim.empty_dict(),\n  id = 'nvim.bufwrite \"minimal.lua\"',\n  source = \"nvim\",\n  status = \"running\",\n  text = { '\"minimal.lua\" ' },\n  title = \"\"\n}\n\nNo subsequent event with status = \"success\" or status = \"failed\" is emitted for the same progress ID.\n\nThis causes my terminal (ghostty) to produce a progress/working indicator at the top of the window.\n\nNo actual write is done to the read file (verified with stat on my filesystem).\n\nSuspected cause according to AI (GPT 5.6 Sol):\n\nI haven't verified this in any way but thought it may help reduce the efforts to investigate:\n\n\n> This appears to have been introduced by commit ff68fd6 (#39280).\n> \n> filemess() is a generic helper used by both file-reading and file-writing code. It now treats an empty s argument as the start of a buffer-write progress operation:\n> \n> ```c\n> if (*s == NUL) {\n>   char msg_id[IOSIZE + 14] = \"nvim.bufwrite \";\n>   xstrlcat(msg_id, IObuff, 14 + strlen(IObuff));\n>   msg_progress(IObuff, msg_id, \"running\", 0, false, true);\n> }\n> ```\n> However, readfile() also invokes it with an empty suffix:\n> \n>```c\n> filemess(curbuf, sfname, \"\");\n>```\n> \n> Therefore, the read path unintentionally starts a nvim.bufwrite progress operation.\n> \n> The actual write path later emits the matching terminal event in buf_write():\n> \n>```c\n> msg_progress(IObuff, msg_id, \"success\", 0, true, true)\n>```\n> \n> The read path has no equivalent terminal update.\n> \n> Commit dda30fd (#39315) later changed the progress ID to include the nvim. prefix and filename. That commit did not cause the generic filemess() classification problem, but it results in the currently observed ID:\n> \n> nvim.bufwrite \"<filename>\"\n> \n> A likely fix would be to avoid inferring a write operation from s[0] == NUL inside the shared filemess() helper. The buffer-write progress start could instead be emitted directly by the write path, or the helper could receive explicit information about the operation being performed.\n\n\n\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nminimal.lua:\n\n```lua\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd(\"Progress\", {\n        callback = function(ev)\n                vim.notify(vim.inspect(ev.data))\n        end,\n})\n```\n\nnvim --clean -u minimal.lua\n:read minimal.lua\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n:read should not emit an unterminated nvim.bufwrite progress event.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1244+g54373e2453\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo, vim 9.2.782\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nmacOS 15.7.8\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nbob",
